 'Test NER consistency signal on OOD instances (cross-domain transfer).\n\n    '\n  priority: P2\n  gpu_budget_hours: 5\n  deps:\n  - exp-003\n- id: exp-016\n  name: signal_ensemble_analysis\n  claim: 1\n  description: >\n    SJ+logprob instance-level correlation and ensemble analysis.\n    (1) Pearson/Spearman correlation between SJ and logprob per-instance.\n    (2) Grid search linear combination alpha*SJ + (1-alpha)*logprob.\n    (3) Logistic regression on all 5 signals as ensemble.\n    (4) Best-of-N selection F1 for ensemble vs individual signals.\n    Dream 2 reviewers BOTH independently flagged this as missing.\n  priority: P0\n  gpu_budget_hours: 0\n  deps: [exp-012, exp-014]\n  note: CPU-only on existing pilot_004 data. Zero cost, high impact.\n  registry_id: exp_016_signal_ensemble\n- id: exp-017\n  name: ner_llama_conll\n  claim: 1\n  description: >\n    LLaMA-3.1-8B on CoNLL-2003 to strengthen cross-model validation.\n    Dream 2 flagged LLaMA only on SciERC is insufficient for model-agnostic claim.\n  priority: P1\n  gpu_budget_hours: 10\n  deps: [exp-007]\n  dataset: CoNLL-2003\n  model: llama-3.1-8b (fine-tune on CoNLL)\n  note: Only if exp-007 shows positive signal and GPU hours available.\n","numLines":241,"startLine":1,"totalLines":241}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Nd96yhFv5U9ZqSPCdbiNWM","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have a clear picture. Let me identify all experiments in the experiment_plan and their statuses:\n\nFrom the experiment_plan.yaml, the experiments are:\n- exp-012: baseline_logprob — has registry_id, DONE\n- exp-013: baseline_exact_match — has registry_id, DONE\n- exp-014: baseline_entity_voting — has registry_id, DONE\n- exp-015: evaluation_metrics_expansion — has registry_id, DONE\n- exp-001: ner_n16_scierc — has registry_id, DONE\n\n\n- exp-002: ner_conll2003 — has registry_id, DONE\n- exp-003: ner_wnut17 — has registry_id, orphaned status in the plan context\n- exp-004: re_diagnostic_analysis — has registry_id, DONE\n- exp-005: temperature_sensitivity_analysis — has registry_id, DONE\n- exp-006: adaptive_compute_demo — has registry_id, DONE\n- exp-007: ner_multi_model — has registry_id, DONE\n- exp-008: re_n16_confirmation — has registry_id, DONE\n- exp-009: capability_stratification — has registry_id, DONE\n- exp-010: selection_curves — has registry_id, DONE\n- exp-011: ner_ood_robustness — NO registry_id, status: planned\n- exp-016: signal_ensemble_analysis — has registry_id, DONE\n- exp-017: ner_llama_conll — status: planned in the yaml, but registry entries show it's already done\n\nLooking back at the experiment_plan in the context, the user wants detailed data for each planned experiment. I see exp-011 and exp-017 marked as planned, but exp-017 actually has completed registry entries, so there's a mismatch between the plan status and what's been executed. I'm realizing the user might want me to generate predicted results for all experiments in the plan under a \"best\" tier scenario, not just the ones marked as planned. Many of these experiments already have actual results, so I need to clarify whether they want imagined data for everything or just the truly unexecuted ones.\n\nActually, looking at the request again—\"对 experiment_plan 中每个 planned 实验展开详细数据\"—I think they're asking for complete imagined predictions across all experiments in the plan, treating this as a thought experiment about what the ideal \"best\" outcome would look like. Each prediction needs a rationale explaining why those results would occur under the best-case scenario.\n\nNow I'm looking at the performance metrics and acceptance thresholds for the best tier: reviewer scores around 3.5-4.0, main acceptance rates of 40-60%, findings acceptance at 75-90%, with specific performance gaps like focal_auroc between 0.10-0.17 and focal_rho between 0.15-0.36. The key constraint is that cross_config_ner_structural_gt_logprob_rate should hit 1.0 with 3 effective claims accepted.\n\nFor each experiment, I need to construct results that would fully validate the paper's core claims under this best-case framing. Starting with exp-012 on baseline_logprob—this is the make-or-break experiment where SJ needs to clearly outperform logprob on NER to establish the fundamental contribution.\n\nFor the remaining experiments, I'm mapping out how each one supports the narrative: exp-013 shows exact match as a weaker baseline, exp-014 demonstrates that voting confidence works as a quality estimator but fails as a selection strategy, and exp-015 expands the evaluation across multiple signals where structural approaches consistently outperform surface-level metrics.\n\nMoving to the robustness checks, exp-001 confirms structural signals remain stable even with limited data, exp-002 shows the pattern holds across datasets though with less headroom on easier tasks, and exp-003 reveals a failure case in noisy domains where the signal breaks down.\n\nNow examining relation extraction specifically: exp-004 breaks down performance by relation type and shows entity errors are the bottleneck, with a significant gap between NER and RE signal strength. Temperature sensitivity in exp-005 demonstrates that my approach is more robust than alternatives across different sampling regimes. Exp-006 provides direct evidence that the selection signal works better than greedy decoding for NER but not RE, highlighting where the correlation-selection gap emerges. Finally, exp-007 validates the findings generalize across different model architectures.\n\nMoving into confirmation and stratification: exp-008 replicates the weak RE signal even with more samples, solidifying the task-complexity boundary. Exp-009 tests whether difficulty-based stratification explains the pattern—it doesn't, but independent proxies like entity count and sentence length hold up. Exp-010 visualizes the core finding: oracle performance grows with ensemble size while selection signals plateau, making the gap explicit. Exp-011 will test whether the structural signal degrades under domain shift but remains positive. Exp-016 is exploring ensemble combinations of these signals.\n\nThe ensemble approach adds marginal gains on NER, strengthening the narrative around structural consistency. Exp-017 then validates across models and datasets—structural judgments show strong correlation even with different architectures and data, which is the model-agnostic evidence I need. Now I'm setting up the YAML output to reflect realistic acceptance thresholds: 40-60% probability, reviewer scores around 3.5-4.0, with AUROC gaps of 0.10-0.17 and correlation gaps of 0.15-0.36 across configurations, assuming all three claims hold and structural consistently outperforms logprob-based selection.\n\nThe key is framing the negative findings—like RE weakness and NER selection failures—as meaningful insights rather than fatal flaws, then building out the detailed predictions for each experiment.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8223,"cache_read_input_tokens":52708,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens"